WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:05.839
Hello.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:08.550
Ready?

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:10.427
Night, take 1. 

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.306
Wow, that's--
that's a great question.

00:00:15.516 --> 00:00:17.559
I was very lucky
to have been born

00:00:17.643 --> 00:00:18.894
when I was born,

00:00:18.977 --> 00:00:21.772
and had the filmmakers

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.775
that were telling stories
to my age group at that time.

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.277
Meaning Steven Spielberg
and George Lucas,

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:29.530
making Star Wars and E.T.,

00:00:29.613 --> 00:00:32.866
and it just felt like religion
watching those movies.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.453
And so it was a beautiful moment
to be awakened in that way,

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:37.371
in a visual form.

00:00:37.538 --> 00:00:40.791
And I think I just started to play
with my dad's cameras.

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:43.544
And it just...
started as a hobby,

00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:44.837
then became an obsession.

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:47.923
And then went,
"Hmm, who's this guy, Spike Lee?"

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.092
And then it started to become,

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:51.301
"Hey, maybe you could
go to school for that."

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:54.179
-All right, let's do it guys.
-Back it up.

00:00:54.263 --> 00:00:56.515
Let's try to straighten him out.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:57.933
There were certain filmmakers

00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:00.352
where I understood
what they were doing,

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:03.772
and it was kind of built into the way
I could understand the image.

00:01:03.856 --> 00:01:07.401
And so, as I was looking at
more formal filmmakers,

00:01:07.484 --> 00:01:09.570
maybe even kind of
older filmmakers,

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:13.407
like Kurosawa and Kubrik,
and Hitchcock,

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.493
their formality spoke to me.

00:01:16.577 --> 00:01:19.246
My mind worked better
when I slowed things down.

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:22.916
So where it wasn't action-y,
and it was more about the dinner table.

00:01:25.502 --> 00:01:29.006
If I take any scene,
and lean its suspense,

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:32.718
and lean it to mystery,
and it feels right.

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:33.802
I don't know what that is,

00:01:33.886 --> 00:01:38.515
why some people are drawn
 to one genre over another.

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:41.101
I love to do drama,
so I think that the two of those

00:01:41.185 --> 00:01:42.477
work really well together.

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:48.442
You know, I take great pride
in the fact that...

00:01:48.525 --> 00:01:49.902
I was born in India.

00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:54.990
America gave my dad a chance
to follow his dream, you know.

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:56.074
He was a doctor.

00:01:56.158 --> 00:01:59.036
He came to the United States,
left his family,

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:00.621
did the immigrant dream thing.

00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:03.540
And this country is an idea,

00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:06.877
and the immigrant
is the symbol of that idea.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.048
And I want to make sure
that people understand

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:14.218
that the thing they like
about my point of view

00:02:14.301 --> 00:02:16.053
is it's-- 'cause, it's a l--
it's different.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.930
And it's from a different place with...

00:02:18.013 --> 00:02:21.642
My grandma believes
in ghosts and spirituality,

00:02:21.725 --> 00:02:22.976
and you know,
it's not a coincidence

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.479
that I made those stories
about what I make them.

00:02:25.604 --> 00:02:28.273
That's the way I grew up,
and that's where my culture is.

00:02:32.778 --> 00:02:36.573
Earlier, I wasn't ready
to commit to thrillers.

00:02:38.784 --> 00:02:42.788
I didn't even find the genre
'til I was 25 with Sixth Sense.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:47.709
My primary genre is thriller,
but I can still do these other things.

00:02:47.918 --> 00:02:49.503
You know, I wrote Stuart Little

00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:52.589
And there was a part of me
that could've done that,

00:02:52.673 --> 00:02:54.925
just gone and done
kids movies, you know,

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.760
and movies for families.

00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:58.011
There was a period of time

00:02:58.095 --> 00:03:00.806
where I was trying to
straddle both a little bit,

00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:04.184
where I wasn't doing children's
movies, pure children's movies,

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:08.105
like Stuart Little, nor was I doing,
you know, my thrillers.

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:10.691
You can drop the act, brother.

00:03:10.774 --> 00:03:12.150
It's just the two of us.

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:14.403
Okay. All right.

00:03:16.822 --> 00:03:19.533
This thing that I love,
that I didn't really acknowledge,

00:03:19.616 --> 00:03:21.535
which was, I like dark humor.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.537
This is something that
I really love doing,

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.165
and I think audiences are ready
to hear that dark humor note.

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:32.629
Servant has a lot of this kind of
inappropriate dark humor.

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:38.010
I love that, and I want it to be
throughout my work from now on.

00:03:43.557 --> 00:03:47.686
Since film school,
I was relating to European cinema,

00:03:47.811 --> 00:03:49.438
and then Japanese cinema,

00:03:49.688 --> 00:03:51.356
and slowly realized,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.359
maybe my pacing
wasn't American necessarily,

00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:56.570
but was coming
from other influences.

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:59.698
It was speaking a certain cadence,
and a certain language,

00:03:59.823 --> 00:04:02.784
and even I have those conversations
with my partners now

00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:04.453
when they go sell my movies.

00:04:04.578 --> 00:04:05.787
"When you're doing the trailers,

00:04:05.996 --> 00:04:10.125
don't make them look like the cadence
of the other movies that are made.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:12.586
Make it minimal and slower,

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:15.547
and make people stop getting on their,
working on their computer

00:04:15.714 --> 00:04:17.591
or looking at their phone,
and they have to look up

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:20.761
because it's rhythmically new to them,

00:04:20.844 --> 00:04:22.054
and they're forced to look."

00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:25.057
There's a reason
that the shot's taking that long

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.018
and there's no music, 
or you're just listening to breathing.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:29.895
And it causes everybody to stop.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.356
It's kind of visual meditation a little bit.

00:04:35.442 --> 00:04:36.693
Okay, rehearsal's up. 

00:04:37.528 --> 00:04:38.695
Quietly, please.

00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:40.864
When we say,
"Oh wow, that's cinematic,"

00:04:40.948 --> 00:04:45.160
what we're saying actually is,
"You really cared about the frame."

00:04:45.494 --> 00:04:48.247
"You really cared about
the visual storytelling."

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:52.668
"Oh, that lighting has a meaning.
It's making me feel something."

00:04:52.751 --> 00:04:56.505
"Wow, the camera's is exactly
the right distance from the subject,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.423
with the exact right framing.

00:04:58.507 --> 00:05:00.843
It's like a painting
or a singular photograph

00:05:00.926 --> 00:05:02.594
that's evoking something in me."

00:05:02.803 --> 00:05:07.015
Again, when we don't say that,
it's because it's generalized.

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:09.768
When we say "cinema",
we're aware that there's--

00:05:09.852 --> 00:05:12.104
care has been taken
for every art form:

00:05:12.187 --> 00:05:16.149
the costumes, the sound, everything,
the blocking, the dance of it,

00:05:16.233 --> 00:05:16.900
all of that,

00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:20.571
has been thought out, and it's evoking
something very specific in us,

00:05:20.654 --> 00:05:23.657
that usually is reserved for cinemas.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.326
Leanne?

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:28.120
Leanne Grayson?

00:05:28.954 --> 00:05:30.289
Hello, Mrs. Turner.

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:35.127
When we watch something
that is generalized,

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.421
and you don't need to look up to know,

00:05:37.963 --> 00:05:40.674
you know, roughly where you are
or what's going on,

00:05:40.757 --> 00:05:43.760
you don't have to commit of yourself
because I didn't commit to you.

00:05:43.844 --> 00:05:45.179
And slowly over time,

00:05:45.262 --> 00:05:48.807
we've seen examples
where that has broken that form.

00:05:48.891 --> 00:05:51.476
And it is hard to look away.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.188
And it's hard to define
what you're looking at.

00:05:56.607 --> 00:06:01.069
For us, as time went on
and the 24 episodes per season

00:06:01.153 --> 00:06:04.656
shrunk down, and it became 20,
and then 18, and then--

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.326
"Oh, wait, 10 episodes per season."

00:06:07.409 --> 00:06:11.997
As the demand has shrunk,
the quality level is rising at the same time.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.958
And here, with Servant,
we halved it again!

00:06:15.042 --> 00:06:18.128
We said,
"We're gonna do half-hour thrillers."

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:22.591
Shh!

00:06:23.008 --> 00:06:24.009
It's just me!

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:27.846
-What the hell are you doing?
-He's gone AWOL, Uncle George.

00:06:29.348 --> 00:06:32.601
We're getting close to
the equation that film has.

00:06:33.018 --> 00:06:37.564
In this situation, if we stay
in the same location like a play,

00:06:37.648 --> 00:06:38.857
it's getting close to--

00:06:38.941 --> 00:06:41.235
you're not just running around
and getting things done

00:06:41.318 --> 00:06:44.404
because you have this laundry list
of things to get done.

00:06:44.613 --> 00:06:47.366
You can actually do this
at a very, very high level.

00:06:55.457 --> 00:06:59.253
After the first season,
I knew roughly where I wanted to go.

00:06:59.336 --> 00:07:02.756
And I was at my house for 4 months,
I just put up a board and I said,

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.591
"I'm just gonna figure out
where it's going,

00:07:04.675 --> 00:07:06.176
and how long it's gonna take there.

00:07:06.343 --> 00:07:08.762
And that will seep into
everything we're doing."

00:07:08.846 --> 00:07:11.765
Every choice, every camera angle,
every casting decision.

00:07:12.015 --> 00:07:14.643
It'll start moving, and you'll feel it
as an audience member,

00:07:14.726 --> 00:07:16.061
"Wow, they know where they're going.

00:07:16.144 --> 00:07:17.604
And I can feel where we are,

00:07:17.688 --> 00:07:20.858
and the GPS inside me
is telling me we're here,

00:07:20.941 --> 00:07:23.193
on the road to the end."

00:07:24.862 --> 00:07:29.783
I'm drawn to knowing
the ending in a long form situation.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.211
When I started the show,
I think that Apple didn't realize

00:07:41.295 --> 00:07:45.007
I absolutely meant it when I said
we weren't leaving this house.

00:07:46.091 --> 00:07:47.759
I think after I handed in
the first episode,

00:07:47.843 --> 00:07:49.720
they were like, "Wow, that was great,
that was all in the house."

00:07:49.803 --> 00:07:51.847
And then the second episode
and the third episode,

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.224
and then the fifth episode,
and then the eighth episode,

00:07:54.391 --> 00:07:55.434
and then the tenth episode...

00:07:55.517 --> 00:07:57.019
And then we started season 2,
and they were like,

00:07:57.102 --> 00:07:57.978
"Are you staying in the house?"

00:07:58.061 --> 00:07:59.771
I'm like, "Yeah, we're staying
in the house."

00:08:04.318 --> 00:08:07.237
Now I've kind of worked out
every episode in my head,

00:08:07.321 --> 00:08:09.489
all the way to the end of the show,

00:08:09.573 --> 00:08:14.578
and how does our world expand,
and being very meticulous about

00:08:14.661 --> 00:08:18.332
how we're expanding the world,
but keeping it essentially in the house.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.835
-We're rolling on this now?
-Yeah.

00:08:22.085 --> 00:08:22.836
Okay, here we go. Ready?

00:08:23.045 --> 00:08:24.880
And... action.

00:08:24.963 --> 00:08:27.132
I get to work with wonderful people,

00:08:27.216 --> 00:08:28.717
that I never get to work with,

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.011
'cause it's so myopic making movies.

00:08:31.094 --> 00:08:33.931
I go and make a movie,
it takes me two years.

00:08:34.056 --> 00:08:37.017
Most of it's in an editing room,
or in a room alone writing,

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:37.976
and storyboarding.

00:08:38.101 --> 00:08:40.229
So that's most of those two years.

00:08:40.312 --> 00:08:43.440
And so you're-- I'm almost isolated
with either one other person

00:08:44.107 --> 00:08:45.901
for a majority of my life.

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:50.280
Making a show, I get to work
with all of these wonderful people here

00:08:50.364 --> 00:08:52.324
and try new people,
try to work with new people,

00:08:52.449 --> 00:08:55.285
learn new things from new people.
I can take risks.

00:08:55.369 --> 00:08:59.206
Um, costume designers,
and production designers, and actors,

00:08:59.289 --> 00:09:03.961
and writers, and editors, and composers,
and it's like film school.

00:09:04.044 --> 00:09:06.046
It's just a wonderful experience for me.

00:09:06.129 --> 00:09:09.132
I'm taking full advantage of it,
and it's affecting my movies.

00:09:13.554 --> 00:09:15.514
It's just a wonderful
experience for me to go

00:09:15.597 --> 00:09:18.725
from this isolationist way
of making my career

00:09:18.809 --> 00:09:22.104
to kind of being with others
and learning and growing.

00:09:22.187 --> 00:09:24.523
We either will do one
of two things as human beings.

00:09:25.023 --> 00:09:28.193
We either calcify or we expand.

00:09:28.277 --> 00:09:30.946
You don't have the option
to stay where you are.

00:09:31.029 --> 00:09:33.615
It's not an option.
That's not... that's not life.

00:09:34.157 --> 00:09:36.577
All right, here we go. Roll, please.

00:09:36.785 --> 00:09:37.953
A only, mark.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:42.416
I love supporting other filmmakers.

00:09:42.499 --> 00:09:45.294
This is an opportunity for me
to grab somebody

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:48.505
who maybe wouldn't have had
this opportunity before

00:09:48.589 --> 00:09:52.176
and giving them the opportunity
and teaching them to follow their voice,

00:09:52.259 --> 00:09:54.845
and showing them
how amazing they are.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.097
And they're doing things
that I could never do,

00:09:57.181 --> 00:10:00.309
because they're doing it
in their own singular voice.

00:10:00.601 --> 00:10:02.102
And... action!

00:10:07.316 --> 00:10:08.650
When we get directors,

00:10:08.817 --> 00:10:11.987
I pick the directors
'cause I saw their film at a film festival

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.614
or I saw their short film
somewhere,

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:15.407
they're from
some country somewhere

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:19.161
and I brought them here
and I said, "Don't change.

00:10:19.244 --> 00:10:21.663
Be exactly as weird and different.

00:10:22.039 --> 00:10:25.334
Don't try to be me,
and don't try to please everybody.

00:10:25.417 --> 00:10:26.835
Be you, specifically.

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:30.130
And I want you to do an episode
that no one else could've done...

00:10:30.214 --> 00:10:30.964
just you."

00:10:31.048 --> 00:10:32.549
I want that specificity.

00:10:32.633 --> 00:10:37.513
And I think hearing someone's voice
unadulterated is a very powerful thing.

00:10:37.596 --> 00:10:38.847
I love that.

00:10:38.931 --> 00:10:41.433
That if we have different voices
from around the world...

00:10:41.517 --> 00:10:43.519
I want Servant to feel different.

00:10:43.602 --> 00:10:45.312
It wasn't a cookie cutter show.

00:10:46.855 --> 00:10:47.898
Dorothy Turner.

00:10:50.359 --> 00:10:51.318
Do I know you?

00:10:51.401 --> 00:10:54.112
They're all people
that have connected with me

00:10:54.196 --> 00:10:55.614
very deeply as artists.

00:10:55.697 --> 00:10:59.034
So you're already starting
at a common language.

00:10:59.117 --> 00:11:01.078
They're not doing things that I don't get.

00:11:01.328 --> 00:11:04.665
I get what they're doing,
they're doing it differently than I'm doing it.

00:11:04.873 --> 00:11:06.750
I'm not asking them
to change who they are.

00:11:06.834 --> 00:11:08.836
I'm really celebrating who they are.

00:11:09.044 --> 00:11:10.254
My hope is, you know,

00:11:10.337 --> 00:11:13.799
I keep on growing
this family of artists.

00:11:13.966 --> 00:11:15.384
It's slightly different than me,

00:11:15.467 --> 00:11:19.555
but it's still in a value system
that is mine.

00:11:20.973 --> 00:11:22.558
Why haven't you told her?

00:11:22.683 --> 00:11:24.518
No, no, no, no. Follow her!

00:11:24.768 --> 00:11:26.311
You follow Leanne!

00:11:26.436 --> 00:11:30.274
What I want for Servant is
to be hard to define.

00:11:30.357 --> 00:11:32.401
You don't know whether to laugh,
whether to scream.

00:11:32.651 --> 00:11:35.904
Is this a farce? Or is this a tragedy?

00:11:36.029 --> 00:11:39.283
As long as we understand
our language and we're very specific,

00:11:39.366 --> 00:11:43.328
we don't speak like anyone else,
you have to listen to us.

00:11:43.996 --> 00:11:45.998
And I believe
when you do art like that,

00:11:46.081 --> 00:11:49.877
you're giving everyone permission
to be individuals.

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:52.963
And to learn about
who they are themselves.

00:11:53.046 --> 00:11:55.716
Nothing matters
if I don't have my son.

00:11:56.008 --> 00:12:00.179
My goal is for this show,
when we get to its last episode,

00:12:00.262 --> 00:12:02.973
we have not lost its quality level.

00:12:03.056 --> 00:12:05.601
In fact, you're gonna know
instantaneously,

00:12:05.684 --> 00:12:08.187
"Wow, there is no show like it. Period."

